    - analysis_quality  # 893f
    isbefore: []  # 893f
    duplicate_of: []  # 893f
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 893f
    pr_ref:  # 893f
    description: "FILED FROM WI-doluf's cross-language check, which the owner asked for before\nchoosing the shape of a fix. The check answered the shape question and turned up\na larger hole than the one it was run for.\n\nTHE DATA. Method-kind io_primitives rows, deduped for catalogue inheritance\n(_CATALOG_PARENTS: kotlin/scala -> java, cpp -> c, elixir -> erlang). Every one\nof these needs a TYPED RECEIVER before it can ever match:\n\n  lang         own method rows   own receiver types   ADR-0006 'Chained'\n  python              184                26           NO  (Phase 4)\n  java                139                36           NO  (Phase 1, never landed)\n  objc                123                18           NOT IN THE TABLE\n  rust                118                26           YES (Phase 3 / WI-titor)\n  swift               100                31           NOT IN THE TABLE\n  javascript           48                13           NO  (n/a)\n  go                   45                16           YES (Phase 1; WI-doluf)\n  kotlin               42                 3           NO  (Phase 2)\n  scala                21                 5           NOT IN THE TABLE\n  TOTAL               820\n\nMethod rows are 97% of java's catalogue, 97% of kotlin's, 95% of objc's, 93% of\nswift's, 89% of scala's. For those languages, receiver typing is not one code\npath among many -- it is the precondition for almost the whole catalogue.\n\ncsharp is the clean negative: it has an analyzer and NO io_primitives catalogue,\nso it needs nothing here. Recording that so the next sweep does not re-derive it.\n\nWHAT IS MISSING, PRECISELY. The registries are built from ANALYSED DECLARATIONS,\nso a receiver typed from a library function's return value cannot be typed at\nall -- there is no declaration in the repo to register. Measured on go:\n`ln, _ := net.Listen(...)` then `ln.Accept()` emits dst go:external:0-0:Accept,\nand the catalogue's net.Listener.Accept row is unreachable no matter how correct\nit is. The missing input is one fact per producing function: what type does it\nreturn.\n\nWHY A SEPARATE CATALOGUE AND NOT A `returns:` KEY ON io_primitives ROWS.\nConsidered and rejected on three grounds, in order of weight:\n\n  1. It is a SIGNATURE fact, not an I/O fact. Putting \"what type does this\n     return\" inside a file whose declared job is \"what boundary does this cross\"\n     is one name carrying two meanings -- the shape INV-tutar cost 134\n     misclassified rows on, and the shape ADR-0049 was written to rule.\n  2. The CONSUMER IS NOT THE IO CATALOGUE. It is `var_types` in nine analyzers,\n     which also feeds slice quality, centrality, dead-code and taint. Scoping\n     the data to io rows would under-serve its own users, and the receiver types\n     an io row needs are a SUBSET of the ones a slice needs.\n  3. ONE table shape serves nine languages; an io_primitives extension is nine\n     parallel additions to nine files, each able to drift.\n\nSHAPE: key it the way the existing return-type registries already are\n(`Receiver.Method` / bare function name -> returned type), so per-language\nmachinery consumes it with NO new resolution path. WI-doluf proved that path end\nto end in go: with the qualifier preserved, an in-repo factory returning net.Conn\nnow classifies net.Conn.Read where it previously matched nothing.\n\nSEQUENCING NOTE. This is NOT the shortest path to unblocking INV-kanuk -- the\nADR-0049 disclosure boundary (WI-nosah) is, and it needs no new data. This is\nthe path that additionally FIXES RECALL: it makes go's, java's and the rest's\nreal receive surfaces reachable rather than merely stopping the false ones from\nminting. The two are complementary and neither waits on the other.\n\nSUB-GAP WORTH ITS OWN LOOK: scala / objc / swift are absent from ADR-0006's\nsupport table entirely -- not marked NO, not marked n/a, absent -- so there is no\nrecorded answer to whether their receivers are typed by any mechanism, while\n244 method rows depend on it. They are now listed 'not assessed' in the ADR\nrather than silently missing. Assessing them is the first step here."  # 893f
